"""Interview questions for TypeScript for Applications and Docker for Developers."""

from app.content._build import qa

INTERVIEW = {
    # TypeScript
    "ts-setup": [
        qa("Why use TypeScript instead of plain JavaScript?",
           "Static types catch whole classes of bugs at compile time (typos, wrong arguments, possibly-undefined values), enable reliable autocompletion and refactoring, and document the code. The output is plain JavaScript, so it runs anywhere JavaScript does.",
           "Large frontends and Node services adopt TypeScript mainly for safe refactoring: renaming a field updates every use or fails the build."),
        qa("What's the difference between `any` and `unknown`?",
           "`any` switches type checking off: you can do anything with the value. `unknown` is the safe counterpart: you must narrow it (with typeof, instanceof or a validation function) before using it.",
           "Data from `JSON.parse` or an API is best typed as `unknown` and validated, for example with Zod, before the rest of the code trusts it."),
    ],
    "ts-types": [
        qa("What's the difference between `type` and `interface`?",
           "Both describe object shapes. Interfaces can be extended with `extends` and merge when declared twice, which suits public object contracts. Type aliases can also name unions, tuples, primitives, and mapped or conditional types. Teams usually pick one style for objects and use `type` for everything else.",
           "Library authors use interfaces so users can augment them; application code often uses `type` for unions such as API states."),
        qa("What is structural typing?",
           "TypeScript compares types by their shape, not their name: an object with the required properties is compatible even if it was never declared as that type. That matches how JavaScript code is actually written.",
           "Tests can pass a plain object literal wherever a `User` is expected, without constructing a class instance."),
    ],
    "ts-unions": [
        qa("What is a discriminated union, and why is it useful?",
           "A union of object types that share a literal 'tag' property, such as `status: 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'`. Checking the tag narrows the whole object, so you can only use fields that exist in that variant, and an exhaustive check with `never` catches unhandled cases.",
           "UI state, Redux actions and API results are modelled this way, which eliminates bugs such as reading `data` while it's still loading."),
        qa("What is type narrowing?",
           "TypeScript refines a variable's type inside a branch based on checks: typeof, instanceof, `in`, equality with literals, truthiness, and user-defined type guards such as `function isUser(x): x is User`.",
           "Handling an API response that may be an error or a result, or a value that may be undefined, relies on narrowing instead of unsafe casts."),
    ],
    "ts-functions": [
        qa("What are generics, and when would you use them?",
           "Generics are type parameters that let a function, class or type work with many types while keeping the relationship between them, such as `first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined`, which returns the element type it was given.",
           "Typed API clients (`get<User>('/users/1')`), collection helpers and components that render lists of any item type use generics."),
        qa("What are utility types such as Partial, Pick and Omit?",
           "Built-in generic types that derive new types from existing ones: `Partial<T>` makes every property optional and `Required<T>` does the reverse, `Pick<T, K>` keeps some properties, `Omit<T, K>` removes some, and `Record<K, V>` builds an object type.",
           "An edit form uses `Partial<User>` and a create request uses `Omit<User, 'id' | 'createdAt'>`, so both stay in sync with the main type."),
    ],
    "ts-dom": [
        qa("How do you prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) in frontend code?",
           "Never insert untrusted text as HTML. Use `textContent`, or frameworks that escape output by default (React, Vue). Escape values when you must build HTML strings, avoid `eval`-like APIs, and add a Content Security Policy as a second line of defence.",
           "This app builds HTML with template strings, so every value passes through its `escapeHtml` helper first."),
        qa("What is event delegation?",
           "Instead of adding a listener to every child element, add one to a common parent and check `event.target` to see which child was clicked. Events bubble up, so one handler covers current and future children.",
           "A list with thousands of rows, or one whose items are added dynamically, stays fast with a single delegated click handler."),
    ],
    "ts-fetch": [
        qa("How do you handle errors with fetch?",
           "fetch only rejects on network failures, so check `response.ok` (status 200 to 299) and throw for HTTP errors yourself. Wrap calls in try/catch, set a timeout with AbortSignal, show users a clear message, and read the error body when the API provides one.",
           "A typed `apiRequest<T>()` helper that does all of this in one place keeps every screen's error handling consistent."),
        qa("What is CORS, and why does it exist?",
           "Browsers block JavaScript from reading responses from another origin unless that server opts in with CORS headers such as Access-Control-Allow-Origin. It protects users: a malicious site can't read your bank's API responses using your logged-in session.",
           "Serving the frontend and API from one origin (as this app does in production), or using a development proxy, avoids CORS entirely; otherwise the API must allow specific origins."),
    ],
    "ts-async": [
        qa("Explain the JavaScript event loop.",
           "JavaScript runs your code on one thread. Synchronous code runs to completion, and finished asynchronous work queues callbacks. Microtasks (promise callbacks) run as soon as the current task ends, before macrotasks such as timers and events, which is why a resolved promise's `.then` runs before a `setTimeout(..., 0)` callback.",
           "This explains why long synchronous loops freeze the page and why heavy computation belongs in a Web Worker."),
        qa("Promise.all, allSettled, race or any: when do you use each?",
           "`all` waits for every promise and rejects on the first failure; `allSettled` waits for all of them and reports each result; `race` settles with the first promise to settle; `any` resolves with the first success. Choose based on whether one failure should fail everything.",
           "A dashboard loading independent widgets uses allSettled so one failed widget doesn't blank the page; a request with a timeout can use race."),
    ],
    "ts-build": [
        qa("What does `strict` mode enable, and why turn it on?",
           "It enables strictNullChecks, noImplicitAny, strictFunctionTypes and related checks. The biggest win is null safety: values that might be undefined must be handled before use, which removes the most common runtime error in JavaScript.",
           "New projects enable strict from day one; teams migrating older codebases often turn it on file by file."),
        qa("How would you set up a production build pipeline for a TypeScript app?",
           "Type-check with `tsc --noEmit`, lint and run tests, then bundle with a tool such as Vite (minified, tree-shaken, content-hashed files), all in CI on every push. Serve the built assets with long cache headers and deploy only after the pipeline passes.",
           "This project's `npm run build` runs `tsc --noEmit && vite build`, so a type error stops the deploy."),
    ],
    # Docker
    "docker-intro": [
        qa("What problem does Docker solve?",
           "It packages an app with its runtime, libraries and configuration into an image that runs the same everywhere, which removes 'works on my machine' problems, speeds up onboarding and makes deployments repeatable. Containers also isolate apps from each other on one host.",
           "A new developer runs `docker compose up` instead of installing Python, PostgreSQL and Redis by hand, and CI runs tests in the same image that goes to production."),
        qa("How does container isolation work on Linux?",
           "Containers are ordinary processes, isolated with kernel namespaces (their own view of processes, network, mounts and hostname) and limited with cgroups (CPU and memory caps). The image's layers form the container's root filesystem.",
           "That's why containers start in milliseconds, and why a kernel vulnerability can affect every container on a host, so hosts still need patching."),
    ],
    "docker-run": [
        qa("What's the difference between `docker run`, `docker start` and `docker exec`?",
           "`run` creates a new container from an image and starts it. `start` restarts an existing, stopped container. `exec` runs an additional command inside a container that's already running.",
           "When debugging, you `exec` into a running API container to inspect files or environment variables, rather than starting a new one."),
        qa("What does `docker run -p 8080:80` mean, and why isn't EXPOSE enough?",
           "It publishes container port 80 on host port 8080, so traffic to localhost:8080 reaches the app. EXPOSE in a Dockerfile only documents which port the app uses; nothing is reachable without `-p` or a Compose ports entry.",
           "Running two versions of a service side by side works by publishing them on different host ports, such as 8001 and 8002."),
    ],
    "docker-images": [
        qa("What's the difference between an image and a container?",
           "An image is an immutable, layered template containing a filesystem and metadata such as the default command. A container is a running instance of an image, with its own thin writable layer, processes and network settings. Many containers can share one image.",
           "Scaling a web service horizontally means running more containers from the same image."),
        qa("Why shouldn't you deploy the `latest` tag?",
           "`latest` is just a default name that moves whenever someone pushes; it doesn't mean newest or stable. Deployments become unrepeatable and rollbacks unclear. Pin version tags or immutable digests (`image@sha256:...`).",
           "Production manifests reference images by commit SHA or digest, so you always know exactly which build is running and can roll back precisely."),
    ],
    "docker-dockerfile": [
        qa("What's the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT?",
           "ENTRYPOINT sets the executable that always runs; CMD supplies its default arguments, or the whole default command when there's no ENTRYPOINT. Arguments after the image name in `docker run` replace CMD but are appended to ENTRYPOINT.",
           'A command-line tool\'s image uses `ENTRYPOINT ["mytool"]` with `CMD ["--help"]`, so `docker run mytool sync` runs `mytool sync`.'),
        qa("Why use the exec (JSON array) form of CMD?",
           "The exec form runs your program directly as process 1, so it receives signals such as SIGTERM and can shut down gracefully. The shell form wraps it in `/bin/sh -c`, which may not forward signals, causing slow, forced stops.",
           "Web servers need SIGTERM to finish in-flight requests during deploys; this app's Dockerfile uses `exec` inside its shell command for the same reason."),
    ],
    "docker-layers": [
        qa("How does the Docker build cache work, and how do you use it well?",
           "Each instruction produces a layer that's cached by the instruction and its inputs (for COPY, the file contents). When something changes, that layer and every later one are rebuilt. Copy dependency manifests and install dependencies before copying source code, so code changes reuse the cached dependency layer.",
           "Ordering a Python Dockerfile this way turns multi-minute rebuilds into seconds, both locally and in CI."),
        qa("What belongs in .dockerignore?",
           "Anything the build doesn't need or shouldn't contain: version-control folders, virtual environments, node_modules, build output, local databases, logs and secrets such as .env files. It shrinks the build context and stops secrets leaking into images.",
           "Without one, a build can send gigabytes of node_modules to the builder and bake a developer's .env file into a public image."),
    ],
    "docker-fastapi": [
        qa("How do you configure a containerized app for different environments?",
           "Build one image and inject configuration at run time with environment variables, env files or the platform's secret store, following the twelve-factor app approach. Never build environment-specific images or bake secrets into them.",
           "The same image runs locally with `--env-file .env` and on Render with dashboard environment variables, as this project does."),
        qa("What's the difference between liveness and readiness checks?",
           "A liveness check asks 'is the process healthy, or should it be restarted?'. A readiness check asks 'can it serve traffic right now?', for example once it has connected to its database. Orchestrators restart containers that fail liveness and stop routing traffic to those that aren't ready.",
           "Kubernetes defines both probes; Docker's HEALTHCHECK and Render's health check path serve a similar purpose, as this app's `/api/health` does."),
    ],
    "docker-volumes": [
        qa("Named volumes or bind mounts: when do you use each?",
           "Named volumes are managed by Docker, portable, and the right choice for persistent data such as databases. Bind mounts map a specific host folder into the container, which suits development (live code editing) or sharing host files, but depends on the host's folder layout.",
           "A PostgreSQL container keeps its data in a named volume, while the API's source code is bind-mounted during development so edits reload instantly."),
        qa("Why should containers be stateless?",
           "If no important data lives inside the container, it can be stopped, replaced, scaled or moved at any time. State belongs in databases, object storage or volumes. That's what makes rolling deploys and autoscaling safe.",
           "Uploaded files go to object storage such as S3, not the container's filesystem, so any replica can serve them and redeploys don't lose them."),
    ],
    "docker-compose": [
        qa("What is Docker Compose good for, and what are its limits?",
           "Compose defines a multi-container app (services, networks and volumes) in one YAML file and starts it with one command, which is ideal for development, CI and simple single-host deployments. It doesn't schedule containers across many machines or recover from node failures; that's an orchestrator's job (Kubernetes, ECS, Nomad).",
           "Teams commonly use Compose locally to mirror production dependencies, then deploy to a managed platform or Kubernetes."),
        qa("How do containers in a Compose project find each other?",
           "Compose creates a network for the project and registers each service's name in its internal DNS, so containers reach each other by service name and container port, for example `db:5432`. Published ports are only for access from the host.",
           "That's why the API's DATABASE_URL uses `@db:5432` instead of localhost, and why the database needs no published port."),
    ],
    "docker-compose-dev": [
        qa("How do you make a service wait until its database is ready?",
           "Give the database a healthcheck (for example `pg_isready`) and use `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy`. The app should still retry its connection with backoff, because databases can restart later, and production platforms have no Compose ordering at all.",
           "Robust services retry their first database connection for a few seconds instead of crashing when they start before the database."),
        qa("How do you get fast feedback while developing inside containers?",
           "Sync or bind-mount the source code into the container and run the server with auto-reload, rebuilding the image only when dependencies change. `docker compose watch` automates both.",
           "Saving a file updates the running API within a second, while everyone on the team still uses identical dependencies."),
    ],
    "docker-multistage": [
        qa("What are multi-stage builds, and why use them?",
           "A Dockerfile with several FROM stages: early stages compile code or install build tools, and the final stage copies in only the results. Production images then contain no compilers, caches or source files they don't need, so they're smaller and have fewer vulnerabilities.",
           "This app's image builds the frontend with Node, then ships only the static files inside a slim Python image without Node or node_modules."),
        qa("How would you reduce an image's size?",
           "Use a slim or distroless base image and multi-stage builds, add a .dockerignore, pass `--no-cache-dir` to pip, clean package-manager caches in the same RUN step that creates them, and don't copy files you don't need. Check the results with `docker images` and `docker history`.",
           "Smaller images pull faster during deploys and autoscaling, cost less to store and have a smaller attack surface."),
    ],
    "docker-security": [
        qa("What are the most important container security practices?",
           "Run as a non-root user, use minimal and regularly rebuilt base images, keep secrets out of images, scan images for known vulnerabilities, drop unneeded Linux capabilities, use read-only filesystems where possible, and never mount the Docker socket into untrusted containers.",
           "Many organisations enforce these with image scanning in CI and admission policies that block root containers or images with critical vulnerabilities."),
        qa("How should secrets be provided to containers?",
           "At run time, through the platform's secret store (Kubernetes Secrets, a cloud secrets manager, Render's environment settings), as environment variables or mounted files, never baked into images or committed to Git. Build steps that need secrets use BuildKit secret mounts.",
           "If a secret leaks into an image or a repository, rotate it immediately: deleting it from the latest version doesn't remove it from history."),
    ],
    "docker-ci-deploy": [
        qa("Describe a typical CI/CD pipeline for a containerized app.",
           "On every push: check out the code, run linters and tests, build the image, scan it, push it to a registry tagged with the commit SHA, then deploy that exact image to staging and, after checks or approval, to production. Rolling back means redeploying the previous tag.",
           "This repository's Render setup deploys on every push to main; adding a GitHub Actions test job would gate that deploy on passing tests."),
        qa("Which deployment strategies reduce risk?",
           "Rolling updates replace instances gradually; blue-green deployments run the new version alongside the old one and switch traffic at once, with instant rollback; canary releases send a small share of traffic to the new version and watch metrics before continuing. Health checks and fast rollbacks matter in all of them.",
           "Large services ship many times a day using canary releases that roll back automatically when error rates rise."),
    ],
}
